## Commands

| When | Run |
|---|---|
| During a design/implementation phase | Compile checks and targeted tests or probes for the scope just implemented. No integrated gate after a slice or commit. |
| After all functional scope in the plan is implemented | `just test-all`: the functional tests (`just check`). Then `just hygiene`, every non-functional check, once; fix what fails and re-run a single check with `just <id>`. Qualification cites both passing for the same tree |
| End of a turn that changed files | The root agent runs `just turn-end` (ADR index, formatting with ruff's safe auto-fixes). Subagents don't |
| After a dependency, toolchain or skill-selection change, or an environment-shaped failure | `just ready` (skills sync, tool check) |
{% if with_rust -%}
| Dependency policy | `just deps`, a `hygiene` check: each declared family (`[workspace.metadata.pins]` in `Cargo.toml`) resolves to one version, family members are pinned exactly, every exact pin, cap or git rev has a `docs/pins.md` row, and cargo-deny checks bans and sources |
{% endif -%}
| Move dependencies to the latest | `just upgrade` (root) or `just upgrade <dir> …` (sub-projects), at your discretion; then the affected tests |
| Decisions | `just adr new <slug> --title "…"`, `just adr supersede ADR-NNNN <slug>`, `just adr revisit`; `just turn-end` regenerates the index; `just adr-lint` runs within `just hygiene` |
{% if with_docs_site -%}
| Documentation changes | `just docs-test` for publisher/resolver changes; `docs-check` (publication) runs within `just hygiene`. First run: `just bootstrap-docs`; preview: `just docs-serve`. No product gate solely for docs. |
{% endif -%}
| Select shared library skills | Edit `.config/library-skills.toml`; then run `just ready` (`just skills-check` to inspect). Skills live in `~/.local/share/library-skills` (`LIBRARY_SKILLS_ROOT` if moved); a library skill never cites this repository, so repo-specific wiring goes here and in `docs/pins.md` |
| Tools present? | `just doctor`, or `just ready` |
| Template changes | `copier update --defaults`, then review the diff like any other change (`.copier-answers.yml` is never edited by hand) |

{% if with_rust -%}
The Rust toolchain is pinned to {{ rust_toolchain }} in `rust-toolchain.toml`. The machine's
rustup default may be nightly, so don't pass `+nightly` or `+stable` to cargo in this workspace.
The repository's `.cargo/config.toml` sets 16 Cargo jobs, `sccache` as the compiler wrapper,
and incremental compilation off so the compiler cache can store workspace crates. Keep Clang
and mold as the linker route. Avoid changing `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, rustflags, or worktrees during
ordinary development because those changes disrupt build reuse. For an isolated diagnosis,
override the config through Cargo's environment variables and report it.

{% endif -%}
Python is {{ python_version }} via `uv` (`.python-version`); run Python tools as `uv run …`.
{% if with_python %}The type checker is **pyrefly**, not pyright or mypy. A subdirectory that is a
separate Python project with its own environment goes in root ruff's `extend-exclude`. {% endif %}The
process scripts are stdlib-only and run without a project environment.

## Writing code against libraries

- Library capability skills are pinned, offline indexes kept under `.claude/skills/` but
  gitignored and versioned outside this repo. They are repo-agnostic: a skill never cites this
  repository; the repository only picks libraries and pins. Use a matching skill **before**
  writing against an API rather than relying on memory, and list it in
  `.claude/skills/README.md`.
- For a library no skill covers, **Context7 is the first stop**, then primary sources. Check a
  skill's or document's version against the locked version (`uv.lock`{% if with_rust %}/`Cargo.lock`{% endif %}) before
  transferring a claim. An empty search result is not evidence that a capability is absent.

### Dependencies

- **Add freely.** Add any library the work warrants without asking: no ADR and no
  `docs/pins.md` row. Use the tool's default specifier — `uv add` (a `>=` floor){% if with_rust %}, `cargo add`
  (a caret requirement){% endif %}.
- **Latest by default.** The resolver picks versions; the committed lockfiles record them and
  the `--locked` gates keep runs reproducible. Move to the latest at your discretion with
  `just upgrade`, then run the tests the move affects.
- **Pin only for a reason.** An exact version, upper cap, git rev or hold-back needs an overt
  reason specific to that dependency — a named breakage, a type-sharing family that must
  resolve to one version, a fork or vendored source, golden or byte-stable output, a parity
  oracle, wheel availability — recorded in `docs/pins.md` with when to revisit it (the
  `pin-check` skill{% if with_rust %}; `just deps` checks that every exact pin, cap or git rev has a row{% endif %}).
  Reproducibility, "already in the lock" and a version entering a key or digest are not reasons.

## Testing rules

- **During implementation, only compile checks and targeted tests.** Validate each new piece of
  scope with a compile check and the focused tests or probes that exercise it. Integrated tests
  (`just check`, `just test-all`) wait until all functional scope in the plan is implemented;
  repeat them only for a failure or a subsequent material change.
- **No formatting mid-work; non-functional checks once, at scope end.** Run `just turn-end` as the
  last step of a turn that changed files, not mid-work. Once all functional scope is implemented,
  run `just hygiene` beside `just test-all` and fix what fails (`just <id>` re-runs one check).
  Don't run hygiene checks during implementation: auto-fixes and findings mid-work move code you
  are still reasoning about.
- **Validators are shared.** Invariant validators are library code, used by both tests and the
  production path. Don't write test-only copies.
{% if with_rust -%}
- Rust tests run under nextest in the release profile, so repeated runs reuse cached optimized
  code. Test data may be fresh, existing, or empty according to the test's purpose.
- **Contract snapshots** (insta), once adopted, run with `INSTA_UPDATE=no`. To accept a change,
  read the `.snap.new` diff first, then run `cargo insta accept`. Never run `cargo insta review`,
  which is interactive. A contract snapshot change is a migration, so say so in the commit.
{% endif -%}
{% if with_python -%}
- Python tests live under `tests/`; pytest runs with `--strict-markers --strict-config`.
{% endif %}
## Working agreements

- **Judgment over evidence ceremony.** Principles state properties; never add probes, tests or
  oracles merely as proof of diligence. Run a check when it resolves a real question.
- **No new alignment linting.** Don't add lints or checks for design alignment; update existing
  checks only when they break. Skills are welcome.
- **Probes go to the evidence folder**, `docs/design_review/evidence/<date>_<topic>/` with a
  README, not to a session scratchpad.
- **Detail over length.** Architecture pages have no line budget: the detail the design needs
  comes before brevity. History belongs in Git, not in the design.

## Reporting

- Report outcomes as `passed`, `failed`, `blocked` (name the missing prerequisite) or `not_run`,
  and give the command that produced each. A mocked provider is never `passed`. Never turn
  outcomes into a percentage.
- Design claims carry a principles §D label (`Proposed` … `Tested` … `Measured`). An unlabelled
  claim is a defect; `Proposed` is not.
- Date every "verified" claim.

## Decisions and reviews

- **Write an ADR** (the `adr` skill) when a change alters a §B decision, chooses between real
  alternatives, or would surprise a future session. Amend the owning architectural section in
  the same commit. To pivot, supersede the old ADR; accepted ADRs are immutable.
- **Keep a current working set** (ADR-0001). Update the owner whose meaning changed instead of
  appending history. When a plan, review, evidence folder or ADR loses its last current consumer,
  carry its surviving obligations forward and delete it; Git holds it
  ([historical recovery](docs/README.md#historical-recovery)).
- **Design reviews** use `design-review` and its declared profiles{% if with_ci_profile %}
  (`design-review-code-intelligence`){% endif %}, usually through the `design-reviewer` subagent. The binding's
  **Reviews in this repository** section owns cadence and tier/purpose selection (ADR-0002).
  Apply the principles and review skill within those bounded review periods; [DESIGN §2](docs/design/DESIGN.md#section-2)
  owns the current decision.
  Ordinary implementation does not initiate a domain-model review. Review depth follows impact
  and uncertainty; flow tracing is optional when it resolves a concrete question.
- **Findings** retain stable source-review IDs. The active plan's findings table owns current
  disposition for scheduled work; unscheduled deferrals stay in the source review with a trigger
  (binding §4). Record the responsible component and closure evidence; link from follow-up
  reviews and STATUS. A decision being accepted does not establish implementation or verified
  closure.

FP-07/A4 execution fit is assessed qualitatively from relevant operations and growth/failure
scenarios, with material tradeoffs explained in plain language. This consideration requires no
numerical estimates, cost models, estimators, runtime cost accounting, execution-planning machinery,
instrumentation, formal cost proofs or additional proof artifacts. Such mechanisms need a separate
concrete functional or operational requirement. Quantitative performance/capacity claims still
require measurements; semantic correctness obligations remain.

## Agent coordination

Use subagents when independent work, context isolation, distinct capabilities or independent
judgment justify the handoff and integration cost. Keep small or tightly coupled tasks with the
root when useful. Follow the [shared roles](.agents/roles/README.md), including model/effort routing,
briefs and escalation. [DESIGN §2](docs/design/DESIGN.md#section-2) owns the current workflow decision.
The root agent retains design, integration and acceptance; no fixed role sequence is required.
`library-research` may write new dated folders under `docs/design_review/evidence/` and the shared
library skill its brief assigns; [its contract](.agents/roles/library-research.md) bounds those writes.

## Git

- Work on `main` in the current working tree for ordinary edits, reviews and spikes. Use a
  separate worktree only when truly parallel agents must edit production code concurrently.
  Clean up any remaining worktrees that are fully merged.
- Commit to `main` in small commits. Each message names the slice and any ADR, and states the
  test outcome. Never push unless asked.
- Never force-push or `reset --hard`.
- `.claude/skills/*` is gitignored except the process skills: `adr`, `design-review`,
  {% if with_ci_profile %}`design-review-code-intelligence`, {% endif %}`plan-design-review`,
  `plan-creation`, `create-plan`, `plan-execution`, `execute-plan`, `handoff`, `pin-check`.
- At the end of a session that changed what's true, run the `handoff` skill.
